{% extends 'AdminInterfaceBundle:Layout:list.html.twig' %}
{% block listpane_content %}
	<section class="dp-paneblock dp-listpane">
		<header>
			<h3 ng-pluralize count="ListCtrl.list.length || 0" when="{{ ng_plural_phrase('adm.ticket_slas.count_slas') }}"></h3>
		</header>
		<article>
			<ul class="dp-item-list" ng-if="ListCtrl.list.length">
				<li ng-repeat="sla in ListCtrl.list" dp-state-mark="tickets.slas.edit.{{ ng_var('sla.id') }}" data-id="{{ ng_var('sla.id') }}">
					<a href="{{ ng_href('tickets.slas.edit', '{id: sla.id}') }}">
						<h4>
							<span class="title-text">{{ ng_var('sla.title') }}</span>
						</h4>
					</a>
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p ng-if="!ListCtrl.list.length" style="padding: 15px;">
				{{ phrase('adm.ticket_slas.no_slas') }}
			</p>
			<div class="dp-add-control">
				<a class="btn btn-default" href="{{ ng_href('tickets.slas.gocreate') }}" ng-class="{'active': isStateActive('.create') }"><i class="fa fa-plus"></i> {{ phrase('agent.general.add') }}</a>
			</div>
		</article>
	</section>

	{{ ng_tpl('AdminInterfaceBundle:TicketSlas:delete-modal.html.twig') }}
{% endblock %}
{% block listpane_help %}
	<header>
		<h1>SLAs (Service Level Agreements) track if tickets are being dealt with quickly enough.</h1>
		<p>You can set response time goals for each SLA; tickets that are close to failing a goal enter a <strong>warning</strong> condition, and tickets that don't meet the goal have <strong>failed</strong>. SLAs can run actions on warning or failure.</p>
	</header>
	<article>
		<section>
			<p>When you create an SLA, you decide whether it tracks:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>time until first response</li>
				<li>time until ticket resolution</li>
				<li>user waiting time until ticket resolution (in total)</li>
			</ul>
			
			<p>You define the amount of time for a <strong>warning</strong> (when the ticket is considered close to failing) and a <strong>failure</strong>.</p>
			<p>Tickets in the warning condition are highlighted in the agent interface with an amber background. Tickets that have failed are highlighted red.</p>
			<p>You can define actions that run automatically at warning and at failure. For example, you might want to increase the <strong>urgency</strong> of a ticket that is about to fail an SLA or assign the ticket to a more experienced team.</p>
	
		</section>
<section>
		<h2><span>FAQ</span></h2>
		<dl>
			<dt><em>Q:</em>Can I have SLAs that only apply during helpdesk working hours?</dt>
			<dd><em>A:</em>
				Yes, use the SLA's <strong>Hours</strong> setting to to choose if time is counted 24x7 (constantly), during default working hours (as you have defined in <strong>Tickets > Settings</strong>), or during custom working hours which apply only to that SLA.
			</dd>
	
		<dt><em>Q:</em>What does the SLA Application setting mean?</dt>
		<dd><em>A:</em>Use <strong>SLA Application</strong> to set which tickets are affected by the SLA:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Apply to all tickets: applies the SLA to all tickets created from now on, but <em>not</em> existing tickets</li>
				<li>Agents manually apply the SLA using a ticket's <strong>SLAs</strong> tab</li>
				<li>Apply to new tickets that match certain criteria: the SLA will apply to tickets that meet <em>any</em> of the conditions you set</li>
			</ul>
		</dd>
	
		</dl>
		

		

</section>
		<section class="related-content">
			<h2><span>Related Content</span></h2>
			<ul>
				<li><i class="fa fa-book"></i> <a href="https://www.deskpro.com/manuals/admin-manual/admin-manual.html#slas" target="_blank">SLAs</a></li>
			</ul>
		</section>
	</article>
{% endblock %}